// axis-types.axreg — the fundamental type vocabulary for the canonical set.
//
// AXREG_LAYERING_VALUE_OWNERSHIP_V1 states that a type name is owned by
// exactly one file: the --reg loader is flat, and declaring the same type
// name in two files is a hard error regardless of load order and regardless
// of whether the two declarations are textually identical.
//
// That rule was documented but never enforced in the files. `axis.axreg` and
// `axis-bridge.axreg` both declared Unit/Bool/Int/Text/Value/Dec/ValueList/Fn,
// so the canonical set could not be loaded at all:
//
//   Registry conflict: duplicate type name 'Unit' —
//     declared in axis.axreg and axis-bridge.axreg
//
// This file now owns EVERY type in the canonical set. `axis.axreg` and
// `axis-bridge.axreg` declare none. It contains zero fns by construction:
// types are content-hashed from their canonical TypeDef, fns carry authored
// identities, and keeping the two in separate files keeps that distinction
// visible.
//
// WHICH DECLARATIONS THESE ARE
// ----------------------------
// Six of the eight shared names (Unit, Bool, Int, Text, Dec, Fn) were
// byte-identical in both files and are unchanged.
//
// `Value` was NOT. The two files declared genuinely different types:
//
//   axis.axreg         type Value = prim value                  id 212f4f49…
//   axis-bridge.axreg  type Value = sum [ AsUnit | AsBool |     id 23945287…
//                                         AsInt  | AsText ]
//
// and `ValueList = list Value` inherited the difference (47b62fb6… vs
// f6673941…). Unifying them is a semantic choice, not a mechanical merge.
//
// The bridge's declarations are the ones kept, because they are the ones in
// use: `axis.axreg` declares ZERO fns, so nothing referenced its `prim value`
// variant, while `axis-bridge.axreg`'s 411 fns reference the sum variant
// throughout. Keeping the bridge's definitions leaves every fn signature in
// the canonical set byte-identical; keeping axis.axreg's would have silently
// re-typed 411 contracts.
//
// NOTE: the lab's `axis-surface-m1-config/m1-registry.axreg` still declares
// `Value = prim value`. It is not part of this canonical set and is not
// loaded alongside these files, so it does not conflict — but the two
// vocabularies do disagree about what `Value` means. That reconciliation is
// a separate concern and is deliberately NOT decided here.

registry axis-types 0.5

// ─────────── primitive types ───────────

type Unit = prim unit
type Int  = prim int
type Text = prim text
type Bool = prim bool

// Value: the sum over the bridge's runtime variants that this grammar can
// express. Used to type genuinely polymorphic bridge fns (io_println,
// io_print, io_eprint, debug_trace) honestly. The arms are what makes an
// implicit widening legal: `type_admits` (validation/core_ir.rs) accepts a
// CCall arg of type T where `in (Value)` is declared iff some arm has exactly
// one payload and that payload is T. So a MISSING ARM IS A REJECTED PROGRAM,
// not a cosmetic omission.
//
// It previously read "the closed, finite typing vocabulary: it cannot grow
// because the bridge runtime cannot produce a value outside these variants",
// listing four arms. That was false in both halves, and the false half is why
// the omission survived: `runtime/value.rs` has TEN variants — Int, Bool, Str,
// Unit, Tuple, List, Ctor, Dec, Float, Bytes — and `type Bytes = prim bytes`
// is declared thirty lines below, in this same file, by a registry that had
// just asserted the runtime could not produce one. Measured cost of the three
// missing scalar arms, before adding them: passing a Dec, a Float or a Bytes
// into any `in (Value)` slot failed with TypeMismatch, while Text/Int/Bool
// passed. Adding an arm cannot break an existing caller — see the identity
// note below.
//
// THREE RUNTIME VARIANTS REMAIN UNDECLARED, AND CANNOT BE DECLARED HERE.
// Value::Tuple(Vec<Value>), Value::List(Vec<Value>) and
// Value::Ctor{tag, fields} are all recursive: their payload is Value, or a
// list of it. This grammar has no recursive types. Writing the honest arm
// `AsList(ValueList)` — with ValueList = list Value — is rejected by the
// loader at load time:
//
//   cannot resolve type(s) [Value, ValueList] — unknown referenced type
//   name or dependency cycle
//
// which is the correct answer to an ill-founded definition, not a bug to work
// around. The consequence is a real and permanent hole: a ValueList or a
// Tuple can never implicitly widen into a `Value` slot. It costs nothing
// today because both already travel as their own declared types
// (`in (ValueList)`, and tcp_listen's tuple is declared `out Value`), so no
// widening site exists. Declare any new fn that must take "a list or a
// scalar" as two fns, not one `Value` fn.
//
// WHY ADDING AN ARM IS SAFE. An arm changes this type's STRUCTURAL identity
// (registry/core05/codec.rs `type_identity` hashes the constructor list), but
// a fn's identity is authored literally and never minted from its signature,
// and a CCall records only that authored `target_identity`. So no fn identity,
// no body hash and no bundle identity moves when an arm is added — verified by
// recompiling the same source against a 4-arm and an 8-arm Value and diffing:
// byte-identical bundles. That is the convenient half. The unsettling half is
// the same fact stated backwards: NOTHING TIES THIS DECLARATION TO
// `runtime/value.rs`, so the two drift silently and no hash moves to say so.
// `tests/registry_value_arms.rs` in axis-codegen-bridge-rs is the guard that
// now fails the build when a variant is added to the enum without being
// resolved here.
//
// WHAT THE MISSING RECURSIVE ARMS ACTUALLY COST. Threading structured state
// through `loop_while` is the clean fix for "a fixed-arity callback cannot
// carry context", and it WORKS — `value_make(Text(host), Int(port), Int(0))`
// through `loop_while(seed, pred, step, Int(10))` runs — but the state has no
// declarable type. The composite must register `in (Value) out Value` while
// its body claims `Int`, leaning on "the registry declares the contract, the
// body declares the lowering". So the recommended pattern is one the type
// system cannot describe. That is the use case that most wants
// `AsList(ValueList)` to become expressible; it is the argument to weigh if
// recursive types are ever put on the table.
//
// DO NOT "FIX" THE FORWARD REFERENCES BELOW. This declaration names Dec, Float
// and Bytes before their own `type` lines. That is legal: the loader resolves
// the type graph order-independently, verified by loading
// `type Value = sum [ AsDec(Dec) ]` above `type Dec = prim dec`. Moving this
// line down changes nothing. What DOES fail is wrapping the arms across lines
// — a type declaration is one line — and that failure reports on this line,
// which has been misread once as a declaration-order problem. Keep it on one
// line and leave it where it is.
type Value = sum [ AsUnit(Unit) | AsBool(Bool) | AsInt(Int) | AsText(Text) | AsDec(Dec) | AsFloat(Float) | AsBytes(Bytes) ]

// TextList: a homogeneous list of Text — e.g. str_split's output. Uses the 0.5
// List type shape (shape_kind 3). Its length/emptiness ops are named
// text_list_len / text_list_is_empty; the unprefixed list_len / list_is_empty
// are the ValueList ones. There are no generics, so each element type needs
// its own list type and its own named ops.
type TextList = list Text

// ValueList: a homogeneous list of Value — used by the M1 iteration primitives
// (foreach, range, flat_map, zip, etc.). Analogous to TextList but over Value.
type ValueList = list Value

// Bytes: opaque byte blob (PrimCode 4). Added by BRIDGE_BYTES_IO_M1 to carry
// content for fs_read_bytes / fs_write_bytes / text_to_bytes.
type Bytes = prim bytes

// Fn: a registry function reference passed as an argument (fn-ref-by-name).
// Per core_ir_spec/axis-core-ir-0.5.md (constant pool): a fn-ref is a typed
// constant pool entry whose defHash is this Fn type and whose payload is
// the target fn's 32-byte minted identity hash. No inline lambda abstraction.
type Fn = prim fn

// Dec/Float: numeric prims used by the value-coercion family
// (bridge_to_dec/bridge_to_float/int_to_dec/…). Mirror of the bridge registry.
type Dec   = prim dec
type Float = prim float

// Handle / HandleList: task handles for spawn / join (tasks.rs).
//
// A Handle IS the (handleId, result) pair. `spawn` returns one with the result
// slot set to Unit; the slot is populated ONLY on termination of the task's
// thread, by `join`. So `join` is HandleList in, HandleList out — the same
// handles, their results filled — and there is no separate result type to keep
// in step with this one.
//
// The pair is also what makes this a real type rather than a comment. List and
// product types are hashed STRUCTURALLY, so `HandleList = list Int` would have
// been byte-identical to IntList below and every list of integers would have
// verified clean as a handle list. `list (product (Int, Value))` collides with
// nothing, so handing `join` the wrong list is a TypeMismatch.
//
// The result is typed Value, not a type variable: workers joined together need
// not agree on a return type, and each result carries its own Value tag.
type Handle     = product (Int, Value)
type HandleList = list Handle

// ─── BEGIN GENERATED: M1_MONOMORPHIC_LIST_VOCABULARY_V1 / types ───
// IntList: a monomorphic list of Int. Distinct type identity, enforced at the
// contract boundary — a IntList where a ValueList is wanted is a TypeMismatch,
// and vice versa. List types are hashed STRUCTURALLY, so this identity is
// `list Int` regardless of the name.
type IntList = list Int

// BoolList: a monomorphic list of Bool. Distinct type identity, enforced at the
// contract boundary — a BoolList where a ValueList is wanted is a TypeMismatch,
// and vice versa. List types are hashed STRUCTURALLY, so this identity is
// `list Bool` regardless of the name.
type BoolList = list Bool

// ─── END GENERATED: M1_MONOMORPHIC_LIST_VOCABULARY_V1 / types ───

// FloatList (hand-written 2026-10-12, Chris approved): a monomorphic list of Float, the IntList pattern; list types are hashed structurally, so the identity is `list Float`.
type FloatList = list Float
